it was a day straight out of a horror  movie  it had rained the day prior and the fog  filled the air of the Blue Ridge  Mountains  as expected I had no signal so my GPS  had stopped working  and I was lost  I had to get to my patient's home but  the directions the family gave me landed  me straight in the middle of a bumpy  dirt road covered by the forest fog  I had no way to call for help and I  worried if my small car would get stuck  in the mud puddles and suddenly I'm  approached by a one-eyed man with  tattoos covering his arms and his right  hand  holding the gun on his hip he threatens  me to leave his property  I paused  this was the moment I had been preparing  for  this was a crucial conversation hey  everyone I'm yesi and on this channel we  talk about minimalism and how to live a  happier more fulfilling life with less  so if you want to hear how I not only  dissueded the city decision so that I  could leave safely but also obtain that  man's help twice that day be sure to  stick to the end to find out and if  minimalism and adopting positive habits  sounds interesting to you be sure to  subscribe for more videos aimed at  helping you live a happier more  meaningful life this book is one that I  have read and reread because I love it  that much but I first read it about four  years ago when I worked as an ICU nurse  and I began to feel stagnant I felt that  it was time for me to do something more  and pursue leadership positions so I  began precepting training for more  critical patients and obtained my  certification in ICU but I knew that all  of these things would be in vain if I  did not work on improving one thing my  communication skills as an introvert who  struggled with self-confidence this  became my focus I read this book slowly  over the course of several weeks and I  did this intentionally because I did not  want it to just be another self-help  book that I just kind of mindlessly went  through I wanted to actually apply it to  my life and I eventually left ICU for  home hospice nursing and working in jobs  that literally face life and death  situations on a daily basis what a  better way for me than to practice  crucial conversations than on the job  but crucial conversations happen every  day lesson one recognizing a crucial  conversation the book starts off by  explaining what is a crucial  conversation because it doesn't have to  be a life and death situation it is one  where three things take place one  opinions vary so maybe there's an  argument or some issue to resolve number  two the stakes are high the outcome of  this conversation will either have  positive or negative effects in your  life this isn't just talking about the  weather here and number three strong  emotions are present you may find  yourself feeling angry defensive or  afraid so for example think an argument  with a spouse asking for a promotion  ending a relationship or resolving an  issue with a co-worker or client the  book defines them as the day-to-day  conversations that affect your life my  conversation with this man definitely  checked all the check boxes but luckily  for me I had already been practicing the  first step in the book which was to  recognize that this was a crucial  conversation because how often do we  enter an argument and immediately let  our emotions rule we either attack the  other person or avoid the conversation  altogether and it all crumbles apart and  this is precisely why I paused and  allowed myself to Exhale before I  responded this way I could respond with  intelligence rather than with emotion  lesson number two focus on what you  really want and know what you don't want  this may sound really simple and obvious  but it is so important in my situation  what I wanted was to get to my patients  home safely what I did not want was to  leave the property without having  accomplished this goal and of course the  most obvious I didn't want anybody  getting hurt knowing what it is that I  wanted to accomplish out of this  conversation allowed me with the right  frame of mind so that this way I did not  either react with fear or aggression it  gave me a sort of visual frame in which  direction to focus the conversation what  this does is it takes the perspective  from the bickering back and forth  between two people or two groups and it  instead focuses it on the issue lesson  number three know your communication  style this book has a short quiz to help  you know your default reactions when  faced with a challenging conversation  for some it's attacking and controlling  for me it had always been avoiding and  withdrawal knowing this about myself I  knew that if I defaulted to apologizing  and cowarding down and leaving I wasn't  going to accomplish my goal I wasn't  going to see this patient that urgently  needed my services and today was not the  day that I was going to back down I had  something that I wanted to accomplish  and I needed this man's help to do so so  my next step was to dissuade the emotion  lesson number four is to step out make  it safe then step back in my first  response was to calmly apologize for  intruding and explain my reason for  being there but I also needed his help  and because he was clearly angry I had  to step outside the situation I had to  see his perspective what was he feeling  was he feeling disrespected or  threatened what was his motive what did  he want stepping outside the situation  for a moment and looking at it as though  I was watching it happen allowed myself  to remain calm while I was speaking to  him because it wasn't about defending  myself or attacking him it was about  getting what we both wanted while  maintaining respect lesson number five  is crucial maintain respect but how can  I maintain respect for somebody that is  clearly showing no respect for me  because if I default to cursing or  allowing my emotions to rule the  conversation I am showing that I have no  control I have no power power can be  de